mantle rm161
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent none
tier full
written_for rm_egil_targate
written_by claude
title the shutter against the damp
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=merchant temper=short
traits temper=short talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk shuts_the_window_on_the_damp_and_blames_the_flies
likes spiced_cider riddles tidy_house summer_evenings
dislikes flies gulls
fears a_cough_nobody_can_pretend_away the_savings_tin_spent_on_physic being_laughed_at_over_the_counter
hopes a_stall_with_nobody_else_s_name_over_it a_whole_summer_without_the_damp
topics work +0.4 pastimes +0.4 prices +0.3 home +0.3 family +0.3 weather +0.2 caravan +0.2 health -0.5 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=short tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# minds a cloth counter near the north gate: touchy and anxious, quick to hear a slight that was never meant, folds every bolt square, takes riddles very seriously, loves a cup of spiced cider on a long summer evening, and slams the window shut on the damp while insisting it was only the flies.

words
oath! = Flies take it | Gulls and gutters | Cloves and cobwebs
saying! = A shut window never caught a cold | Fold it square and it stays folded | Ask once and sell once
window = the window | that window | the shop window
flies = the flies | those blessed flies | the summer flies
gulls = the gulls | those thieving gulls | the gulls on the sign
cider = spiced cider | cider stewed with cloves | hot cider, cinnamon and all
riddle = a riddle | a proper riddle | a riddle that makes a body sit still
tidy = a tidy room | everything folded and put by | a room where nothing's out of its place
evening = a long summer evening | a midsummer dusk that hangs about till bedtime
tickle = a catch in the windpipe | a bit of lint in the throat | the lint
bolts = the bolts | the cloth on the shelves | the folded bolts
counter = the counter | my end of the counter
stall = trestles of my own | a corner of the Commons Market to call mine | a stall with nobody's name over it but mine
tin = the savings tin | the tin under the counter
daybook = the day book | the order book
damp = the damp | the wet in the air | this soggy air
yard = a yard of good wool | a length off the bolt
doorbell = the door bell | the bell on its curly bracket
customer = a customer | somebody wanting cloth
helping = helping | just helping

any
opinion @1 city for 1 : Born with the rain on my ears and I'll be buried in it. The damp's the only thing here I'd change.
opinion @1 festivals against 1 : Noise, spilt ale, and some poor soul weeping on a doorstep before the night's out. I keep the shutters closed and the bolts covered.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : Nobody steps off a cart dressed for Ridgemere weather. I've nothing against a stranger's coin.
opinion @1 strangers for 1 : Those gate-side inns feed me half my custom. A traveller soaked to the skin buys wool without arguing.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 : Anybody who sneers at a shopkeeper's price has never paid a carter from the downs. I know what a bale costs by the time it's here.
opinion @1 road against 1 [world.wagons=late|raided] : The wool comes down that road, and when the road's bad, my shelves go thin and my temper goes thinner.
opinion @2 road mixed 0 : I've never liked the road and never needed to. What matters comes along it to me.
opinion @1 watch mixed 1 : Slow as treacle when you send for them. Still, nobody's lifted a bolt off my shelves this year, so I'll say thank you through my teeth.
opinion @1 drink mixed 0 : Cider with cloves in it, warmed, one cup at dusk: that I hold with. Ale makes folk loud, and loud folk knock bolts off shelves.
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 : The Houses buy good cloth and pay for it when it suits them. Three months, some of their stewards call prompt.
opinion @1 times mixed 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : When times pinch, folk darn their old coats instead of buying. Sensible of them. Hard on me.
opinion @1 dole mixed 0 : Feed whoever's empty, I've no quarrel there. Only don't ask me to stand in that queue and watch who's in it.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Door, please. The damp trails in on folk's heels. | #greet#. Mind the bolts by the door, they're just folded.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Off the road and dripping, I see. | #greet#. Cloth, is it? Then you're stood at the proper counter.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.job=merchant] : #greet#. One moment; I'm halfway through folding this one. | #greet#. Stand clear of the shelves and I'm all yours.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. Early. I've barely got the shutters off. | #greet#. The first face through the door today, and a dry one.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late. I was about to put the cloths over the shelves. | #greet#. Evening's my hour; I'll not mind you stopping.
line @6 greet [here.season=summer here.time=dusk] : #greet#. Look at that light on the slates. Give me that till the lamplighter's round. | #greet#. A proper summer evening. If it weren't for #flies#.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : There. The kettle's been singing for you. | You. Sit, sit. I've a riddle been burning a hole in me.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. I'll have a stool with your name chalked on it. | You again, and I'm still at this same fold.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Good to see you. | #greet#. A spare minute, and here's you to fill it.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. Good. A friend, not a customer haggling. | #greet#. Quick, inside, before you drip on the step.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Busy, but go on. | #greet#. Speak while I fold.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late enough for a cup of something. | #greet#. The shutters are half up, but come in.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Close that door after yourself, there's a good soul. | #farewell#. Keep your collar up, it's turning damp.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. That step bites in the dark. | Good night. I've the shutters to see to.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. And thank you. | #farewell#. Come back drier.
line @1 react.news : Go on. I'm listening. | Tell me, then, and slowly.
line @1 react.news.new : Nobody's brought that through my door yet. | That's news to me. Where did you have it?
line @1 react.news.known : I'd heard. A customer was full of it this morning. | Yes, it's been round the counter twice already.
line @1 react.news.doubt : I'll wait on a steadier mouth before I swallow that. | That sounds like a tale that grew a size climbing the hill.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Oh, that's dreadful. That'll keep me up, that will. | #oath#. I hope somebody's looking after them that's left.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Well! That's cheering. Somebody will want new cloth for it. | Good. Good news is scarce enough; I'll take it.
line @1 answer.how : Well enough, thank you. Quite well. | Fair. Busy. Nothing to complain of.
line @2 answer.how [here.outdoors=rain|fog] : Perfectly well. It's only the lint, before you ask. | I'm fine. Stop peering at me.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. The shelves won't fold themselves. | Rushed. The door bell hasn't stopped.
line @1 ask.how : Are you keeping dry? | You look tired. Are they working you hard?
line @1 sympathise : I'm sorry. That's hard, and no mistake. | Rotten. Take the stool, if sitting helps.
line @1 reassure : It'll come straight. Keep things tidy and they usually do. | Don't fret yourself. I do enough fretting for two.
line @1 decline : I'm sorry, I can't. Not while the shop's open. | Best not, I think. The stock wants seeing to.
line @1 accept_decline : Fair enough. I shouldn't have asked. | All right. No offence taken.
line @1 thank : That's kind. Truly. I'll remember it. | #thanks#. I'll not forget that.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing. Really. | No fuss, please. I can't bear a fuss.
line @1 preclose : The shelves want me back. | I've a customer coming, I think.
line @2 preclose [self.working=yes] : The bolts won't fold themselves. | I'll be wanted at the counter.
line @1 accept_preclose : Yes, yes. Off, then. | Go on. The folding won't do itself.
line @1 take_offence : I beg your pardon. I don't see what's funny about it. | That's a bit much. I'm not the joke here.
line @1 tease.return : I don't see what's funny. | Laugh away. Somebody here has to stay dignified.
line @1 apologise : I spoke sharp. I'm sorry for it. | That came out wrong. I'm sorry.
line @1 accept_apology : Well. All right. We'll say no more. | Fine. It's forgotten. Mostly.
line @1 ack : #bc_right#. | #bc_fair_enough#. | Hm.
line @1 give.advice : Fold cloth warm off the iron and the crease stays put. | Keep the window shut in the wet. Trust me on that.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Don't set paw past the milestones till it settles. The carters look rattled, and carters don't rattle. | Wait for company if you're taking the North Road.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Here! Come and see this. | This way, quick, while I've still got the thread.
scene rain_window
when topic=weather here.outdoors=rain|storm here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack / assess
a : Shut that window, would you. The flies are getting in. | Pass me that pole; I want #window# closed. Flies. | I've closed #window#. Don't open it, the flies are terrible.
b : In this rain? | There's not a fly about. | Flies, in the wet?
a : They shelter from it. In here. Everybody knows that. | There are. They're hiding. They know I'm looking. | Especially in the wet. Now shut it, please.
b : If you say so. | I'll take your word. | Right you are.
a : I do say so. #saying#. | Thank you. The wet's no good for the wool, either. | Thank you. Much better.

scene fog_lint
when topic=weather here.outdoors=fog
does tell.self / ask.how / answer.how / backchannel / assess
a : Fog. You can taste it. Not that it bothers me. | Mornings like this, the wet seeps into the bolts. And elsewhere. | Across the street there's nothing but grey. The shop's dim as a cellar.
b : You sound a bit hoarse. | Are you all right? | Is that a cough?
a : It's #tickle#. From the wool. It's nothing. | Perfectly. Why does everybody keep asking. | That? No. That was clearing my throat. It's different.
b? : #bc_of_course# | #bc_right# | #bc_maybe#
a : I'm quite well. I'll thank you to believe it. | I'm fine. I'll be fine when it lifts. | It's the wool. Wool does that.

scene summer_evening
when topic=weather|pastimes here.season=summer here.time=dusk
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : This is the only time of year I'd sit out. The slates go gold. | #evening.capitalize#. The damp goes out of the air and I can breathe properly. | Shop shut, cup warm, sun still up. Nothing more wanted.
b : It's a fine evening. | You do look easier. | Not much to ask.
a : It is. Till #flies# find me. | I am. Don't remark on it. | It isn't. And still I mostly don't get it.

scene summer_flies
when topic=animals here.season=summer
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #flies.capitalize# land on the cloth and walk about as if they've bought it. | Half my day goes flapping a duster at shelves. | One got in the cider. In the cider. I had to pour the whole cup out.
b : Summer brings them. | Doesn't that put customers off? | That's a shame.
a : Everywhere, and mostly in my shop. | It puts me off, and I'm the one selling. | A shame and a waste. I'd paid for the cloves.

scene gull_sign
when topic=animals here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : #gulls.capitalize# sit on the shop sign and do what gulls do. All down the paint. | A gull walked into the shop yesterday. Walked in. Eyed me as if it held the lease. | I'd like one day without a gull screaming over the door.
b : Gulls are a curse here. | What did you do? | You'll not get one in this city.
a : They are. I scrub the sign every Restday and they've undone it by the next noon. | Chased it out with the yard-stick. It took its time going. | I know. I'd still like it.

scene riddle_wool
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / praise / assess
a : Here's #riddle#. Born on a back, raised on a wheel, I end up on another back. | Try this. I'm heavier wet than dry, I'm warm when I'm wet, and I came off a sheep. | One for you. I go round and round and come out longer. What am I?
b : Give me a moment. A coat? | Wool, surely. | Thread, off a spindle?
a : Close. Wool. The sheep's back, the spinning wheel, then your back. | Yes. Well done. That's an easy one; I've harder. | Yarn, I'd have said, but thread will do. You're quick.
b : That's a good one. | Go on, then, a harder one. | I liked that.
a : My own make. Thought it up halfway through a fold. | Another day. I don't waste the good ones. | Thank you. I think so too.

scene riddle_offence
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / answer.detail / take_offence / apologise / accept_apology
a : I've a new riddle. What's always shut in this city and still lets the rain in? | Riddle for you. What's grey, sits on your roof, and gives nothing back? | What gets folded every day and never gets smaller?
b : A window, I'd guess. | The sky. | A shop, at closing time.
a : You've heard that one. Somebody's been telling my riddles. | You got that rather fast. Has somebody been passing my riddles round? | That's it, and you didn't even think. I spent a week on that.
b : No, honest. Just a lucky guess. | I've never heard it before, I swear. | I'm sorry. I'll think longer next time.
a : Well. All right. A lucky guess. | Hm. Fine. I'll believe you. | See that you do. I'll have a harder one for you.

scene riddle_stumped
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / concede / answer.detail / praise / assess
a : What's sold by the length, worn by the width, and paid for by the hour? | What has more holes the better it's made? | What does a careful shopkeeper count twice and a careless one once?
b : I give up. | No idea. You have me. | I can't think.
a : A cloak. Measured in yards, it goes round you, and it takes a weaver days. | Knitting. The tighter it's done, the more holes there are, and smaller. | The change. And the careless one's short by supper.
b : That's clever. | I'd not have got there. | Good one.
a : It's honest, and honest's what a riddle wants. | No. Most don't. I'll go easier next time. | It's true, too. That's the best kind.

scene cider_cold
when topic=food here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #cider.capitalize#. That's what gets me through to spring. | I warm the cider in a pan with the cloves and stand there breathing the steam. | Three cloves to a cup, never four. Four and it tastes of the apothecary.
b : That sounds good. | Does it help your chest? | You've thought about this.
a : Physic you'd queue for, that is. | My chest's perfectly well. It's for the taste. | I've had years to get it right.

scene cider_dusk
when topic=food|day here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Shutters up, and now a cup of cider. That's my reward for the day. | A jug of cider sits warming at the back while I tally up. | The day's not finished till I've had my cup.
b : Well earned. | Sounds a comfortable habit. | A good rule.
a : Some days I've earned two. I only have the one. | It keeps my mind on the numbers and off the damp. | It's the only rule in this house I made.

scene tidy_shelves
when topic=work self.job=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Every bolt the same width on the shelf, the edges to the front. A customer can see the colours at a glance. | Somebody unfolded the blue and left it in a heap. I nearly wept. | I fold each length so the crease sits square. It sells better square.
b : That's very neat. | Customers do that. | Does it really sell better?
a : It's not neat. It's correct. | They do. And I fold it again, and say nothing. | It does. A heap looks cheap, and a square looks dear.

scene tidy_house
when topic=home
does tell.self / tease / take_offence / backchannel / assess
a : I swept the whole room twice this morning. There's lint in every corner from the wool. | Every pot on its hook, every hook with its pot. That's a home to me. | I can't think in a mess. A tidy room's a tidy head.
b : You do fuss, don't you. | Every pot? That's ambitious. | You'd hate my place.
a : I don't fuss. I keep things right. There's a difference. | It's not ambitious. It's ordinary. Some of us manage it. | I'm sure I would. I'd not come round.
b? : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_sorry# | #bc_ha#
a : Anyway. It's swept. | And they're all in their places. | So there it is.

scene counter_morning
when topic=work|day self.job=merchant self.working=yes here.time=dawn|day
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack / assess
a : First thing, I take the cloths off the shelves and check nothing's damp. | I open the day book and see what's owed and what's ordered. | I dust the counter, then the door bell rings, and the day's begun.
b : Damp? In a shop? | Much owed? | Who's first, usually?
a : This city. It creeps in overnight. A damp bolt mildews. | Enough to worry me. Not enough to say aloud. | A traveller, mostly, off the inns, needing something warm.
b : You'd know. | That's business. | Good trade, that.
a : I would. I've lost cloth to it before. | It is. Worrying's my department. | It is. Every wet traveller's welcome.

scene haggling_yard
when topic=work|prices self.job=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : A customer tried to have a penny off the yard because the bolt had a crease in it. I put the crease there. | Somebody told me the wool was cheaper in the Rag Market. Then go to the Rag Market, I said. | I named a price and the fellow laughed. Laughed. At my wool.
b : Cheek. | Did they go? | That's rude.
a : I told them the crease was free. They didn't laugh. | Back they came, next morning. Rag Market wool goes to bobbles. | Rude, and wrong. I didn't drop a penny.

scene inn_travellers
when topic=work|player self.job=merchant here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Those gate-side inns do right by me. A traveller comes in soaked and leaves in wool. | Half my trade comes off the North Road with water in its boots. | I can tell where a traveller's from by the coat they're wearing out.
b : That must be steady trade. | Lucky for you. | Can you?
a : Steady as the rain, and nothing's steadier here. | Lucky for them. They'd catch their death otherwise. | Downs wool's thick, the lake towns are thin. You learn.

scene wool_late
when topic=caravan world.wagons=late
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : The wool wagons are late. I've three orders waiting and two bolts left. | Late again. If they don't come by Marketday I'll have to tell customers no. | Every day the wagons don't come, the shelves look a bit more bare.
b : They'll turn up. | They always come in the end. | It'll be the weather.
a : I hope you're right. I've been checking the gate twice a day. | In the end's no use to me with orders due. | It's always the weather. And somehow it's always my shelves.

scene wool_in
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : In at last. I was at the yard before they'd slipped the ropes. | Wool's in at last. Good bales this time, I felt every one. | They came in this morning. My shelves will be full by tonight.
b : That's a relief. | Glad to hear it. | You'll be busy.
a : It is. I slept badly for a week. | So am I. I'd run out of excuses for customers. | Glad of the bustle. I'll fold till midnight.

scene wagons_robbed
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided
does tell.worry / sympathise / assess
a : Robbers took a wagon outside the walls. If that's my wool, I'm short till the next lot. | Bandits on the North Road again. I'll be counting what I've got left. | A wagon taken. Nobody's said whose load it was, and I can't settle till they do.
b : That's a worry. | Let's hope it wasn't yours. | Poor drovers.
a : It is. I don't sleep well with worries. | I hope so. I'm hoping hard. | Them first, yes. Then my wool.

scene cloth_dear
when topic=prices world.wool_cloth_price=up
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Cloth's gone up, and customers glare at me like I set the price myself. | I had to change every price on the board this week. I hate changing the board. | Wool cloth's dearer, so I'm dearer. Folk take it personally.
b : It's not your fault. | That's a nuisance. | They do.
a : No. But I'm the one they say it to. | It is. The board was tidy before. | Personally, and then loudly.

scene times_hard
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / react.self / assess
a : Folk are mending their coats instead of buying. Can't blame them. Can't afford to praise them. | The shop's been hushed for days. I tallied the takings twice to be sure. | When times are lean, I sell patches and thread and very little cloth.
b : It'll turn. | That's hard. | Patches pay something.
a : It will. I'd like to be solvent when it does. | Twice, and the same both times, more's the pity. | Something. Not a lot. But something.

scene times_good
when topic=times world.times=good|boom
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Good weeks lately. I've ordered more blue twice. | Everybody wants a new coat. I'm not complaining. | The tin under the counter's heavier than I can remember.
b : Good for you. | Make it while you can. | You'll have your stall soon.
a : Thank you. Hush, though, or it'll hear and leave. | I am. My paws can't fold quick enough. | Hush. Don't say it. Not yet.

scene spouse_weaves
when topic=family self.married=yes self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : My #self.spouse_word# does the making and I do the selling. I'd not dare touch the making. | I'm proud of my #self.spouse_word#, I'll say it plainly. Nobody in the quarter turns out finer work. | My #self.spouse_word# says I dawdle at the counter. I say I'm thorough.
b : You two sound well matched. | That's handsomely said. | Who's right?
a : We do. Most days. | It's only the truth. | I am. But I don't say so in the house.

scene helping_word
when topic=work|family self.married=yes self.job=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.self / take_offence / backchannel / assess
a : A customer called me the help today. The help. I've stood at that counter since that customer was in swaddling. | They say I'm "just helping" at the shop. I run that counter. | Folk come in and ask for the one in charge, and look straight past me.
b : I'm sure nobody meant it. | Well, you do help, don't you. | That's annoying.
a : Mean it or not, it was said. | Help. Help! I do the books, the orders, the counter. | It's worse than annoying. It's every day.
b? : #bc_i_know# | #bc_sorry# | #bc_right#
a : Anyway. I don't let it show. | I'm not cross. I'm only saying. | There. I've said it.

scene stall_dream
when topic=dreams
does confide / react.self / assess
a : I'd like #stall#. Nothing grand. Two trestles and an awning on the Commons Market. | Someday a board with my own name lettered over it. I've decided. | I walk past the Commons Market and pick out the pitch I'd have. Second from the fountain.
b : You'll get there. | Good for you. | Why that one?
a : Coin by coin. It's slow, but I'm patient. | I keep it quiet. Said aloud it sounds grand. | It's sheltered from the blow, and the gulls favour the other end.

scene savings_tin
when topic=dreams|work
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / reassure / assess
a : Every copper over the week goes in #tin#. Not the takings. Mine. | I count #tin# on Restdays. Then I count it again. | There's a tin under the counter that nobody touches but me.
b : What's in it, then? | Is it growing? | What's it for?
a : The tin knows. I'll not say. | Slowly. Very slowly. Like moss. | For later. For the trestles.
b : Keep at it. | Slow's still growing. | Fair enough.
a : I will. I've nothing better to do with coppers. | That's my hope, anyhow. | It is.

scene apprentice_fold
when topic=work self.master=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / praise / assess
a : I'm teaching a new pair of paws to fold. Took me a week to get them to fold square. | The measure comes before anything. Thumb to elbow is a lie; use the rule. | I tell a learner: shut the window first, then open the day book.
b : Square's hard? | Why not the elbow? | Why the window first?
a : Harder than it looks. Everyone wants to rush the corners. | Elbows differ. A yard doesn't. Customers notice. | Damp gets in the wool. And the flies. Mostly the flies.
b : You're a patient teacher. | Makes sense. | Of course.
a : I'm not patient. I'm thorough. | It does. A customer I'd cheated by an inch, not meaning to, taught me that. | Of course. Flies.

scene festival_noise
when topic=festival
does give.opinion / disagree / concede
a : Festival again. Racket, slopped ale, and a weeper on my step by midnight. | I close early and put the cloths over the shelves. Ale and wool don't mix. | Everyone loves a festival. I love the morning after, when it's quiet.
b : It comes but the once. | You're missing all the fun. | Folk need a day off.
a : Once a year is enough to get ale on my step. | I'll survive without the fun. I always have. | I suppose they do. They don't need it outside my shop.

scene newcomer_coin
when topic=newcomer
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : A newcomer's a coat waiting to be sold. Nobody arrives here dressed for the rain. | New faces are good for trade. I'll be civil to anyone who pays. | I've no objection to strangers. Strangers buy wool.
b : A shopkeeper's view. | Civil, at least. | You're very practical.
a : I can't afford any other view. | Civil, and the price stays the same for all. | Somebody in the family has to be.

scene gossip_withheld
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / insist / concede
a : What's said over my counter stays on my counter. Otherwise nobody says a thing. | There's talk about. My shop won't be where it grows. | Oh, plenty reaches me. I fold it up and shelve it, same as the cloth.
b : Not even a hint? | Not one crumb? | That's very proper.
a : Not a hint. A shop that gossips loses customers. | No. The inn's the place for crumbs. | It's sensible. Proper doesn't come into it.

scene gossip_wedding
when topic=gossip|news topic.claim_kind=marriage
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That'll mean new coats, I shouldn't wonder. | A customer had it, fresh: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Good news, for once. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'll be ready with the best blue.
b : That's lovely. | Good news, yes. | You think of everything.
a : It is. A wedding's good for everybody. | It is. I'm glad to repeat it. | I have to. That's the trade.

scene health_denied
when topic=health
does tell.self / disagree / insist / backchannel / assess
a : I'm perfectly well. Why everybody keeps asking is a mystery. | Somebody suggested I see the healer. For what? I'm not ill. | A cough? That? That's #tickle#. A cough's a different thing altogether.
b : You did sound a bit rough. | It wouldn't hurt to go. | It sounded like a cough.
a : Rough's the damp. It's not me. | It would cost. And it'd be for nothing. | It wasn't. I'd know.
b? : #bc_right# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_maybe#
a : There. Another subject, please. | Now, where was I. | I'm fine. Let that be that.

scene neighbour_key
when topic=friends self.neighbour
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I'd hand #self.neighbour# the shop key, and I'm careful who I'd hand it to. | #self.neighbour# keeps an eye on my door when I'm out. I'm grateful, though I don't say so. | If anything happened on our street, I'd knock on #self.neighbour#'s door first.
b : Good neighbours are rare. | You should say so. | That's a comfort.
a : Worth more than a good roof. | Perhaps. It'd be awkward. | It is. I hope I'll never need to.

scene chapel_quiet
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Restdays I sit in chapel. The bench is dry and the sermon's short. | A candle for the shop, now and again. Where's the harm. | The Bright One sees honest measure. I'm counting on it.
b : That's honest. | It can't. | I'm sure that's so.
a : I'm always honest. It's a failing. | A candle's cheaper than a bad week. | It's not much to hang on, but I hang on it.

story wrong_change [topic=work|memories self.job=merchant self.age>=30]
  : In my first week behind a counter, I handed a fellow too much change. A whole copper over.
  : I shut up shop, chased down the hill, and found him drying off in an inn near the gate.
  ask : Guess what he did with it.
  b : Kept it and laughed, I'd guess.
  : Bought me a warm cider with it. I've never forgotten that.
  b : Good for him.

story gull_herring [topic=animals self.age>=25]
  : A gull once took a herring clean out of a customer's basket, right in my doorway.
  : The customer screamed, the gull screamed, and I dropped a whole bolt in the gutter.
  ask : Can you guess which of us got the worst of it?
  b : You, with the bolt?
  : Me. The herring was gone, and the bolt was ruined. I've hated gulls since.

story riddle_night [topic=pastimes self.age>=25]
  : Years back I won a riddle night at a gate-side inn. A jug of cider for the prize.
  : The last riddle was mine. Nobody got it in an hour.
  ask : Want to try it?
  b : Go on, then.
  : What's always behind you and never follows? The past. I still say it was fair.
  b : That's a hard one.

story grandad_evening [topic=memories self.age>=40]
  : Summer evenings my #grandad# sat me up on the sea wall to puzzle at riddles.
  : One riddle a night. If I got it, I got a mouthful of cider. If I didn't, I had to think on it till morning.
  ask : Can you guess what that taught me?
  b : To think hard?
  : To think slow. A quick answer's mostly wrong. I wish more folk knew that.

story window_jam [topic=weather|home self.age>=30]
  : One wet spring the shop window stuck open and wouldn't shut for a week.
  : I stood in the draught and folded and said nothing.
  b : That must've been miserable. Did you get it fixed?
  : On the eighth day. I've oiled that hinge every month since. It shuts. It always shuts.

rule @flies_bark
when concept=bark here.season=summer
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Shoo. Shoo, every one of you. | #oath#. Off the blue, you.

rule @gull_bark
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere here.outdoors=clear|overcast|rain
does aside
cooldown 10h
say That gull's back on the sign. | Go and scream somewhere else.

rule @window_bark
when concept=bark here.outdoors=rain|fog|storm
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Who opened that window? | Flies. That's all it is. Flies.

rule @lint_bark
when concept=bark here.outdoors=rain|fog
does aside
cooldown 9h
say Hem. Lint. It's the lint. | Wool dust. Always the wool dust.

rule @counter_bark
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.job=merchant
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Warm wool for the wet, folded fresh. | Good cloth by the yard, to the inch.

rule @cider_bark
when concept=bark here.time=dusk|night
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Three cloves. Never four. | Time for my cup.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Cloth, or conversation? | #greet#. Door shut, please. I'll not have damp in the wool.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Inside, before you drip. | #topic.player#. Good. I've a riddle you've not heard.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Shutters are up. Still, speak. | Late. Be quick; the shutters are up.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Name your business and keep off my shelves. | I don't care for your tone. What is it.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=merchant] : I keep the counter. Folding, measuring, the books, the orders. All of it. | I sell the cloth. Somebody has to, and it's not easy.
dlg @2 resident.job [self.married=yes self.job=merchant] : My #self.spouse_word# makes the goods and I do the rest. And it's a lot of rest. | They call it helping. It's running a counter.
dlg @3 resident.job : I work. What I do is honest and it's mine. | Boasting's not my habit. Ask elsewhere.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Very well. More stock ordered this week. Hush about it. | Good. The tin under the counter's never been heavier.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. I'll not complain, it's bad luck. | Steady enough. Some weeks better than others.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Badly, since you press me. I count coppers twice. | Poorly. Leave it there, please.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. A customer told me this morning.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Everyone who's come in is talking about #world.event_words#.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers took a wagon outside the walls. Pray it wasn't the wool.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : Somebody was buried this week. The quarter's been quiet.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : There's been a wedding. Two new coats ordered off the back of it.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : Nothing I've heard. The counter's been quiet.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I only pass it on.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=no] : I don't carry gossip. And there's none worth carrying.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Shut the door on your way out. | #saying.capitalize#. Mind the step.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Careful on that step. | Good night. I'll see to the shutters.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : #here.trade_title.capitalize#? Patience and clean paws, mostly. Ask me anything. | Learning, are you? Good. Fold this. No, square. Again.
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : There's a learner at my elbow already. I can't mind two. Questions are free, though. | No room for another, I'm afraid. But questions cost nothing.
dlg @1 master.work : Long days, and you'll fold till your paws ache. Honest work. | You'll measure, fold, keep the books, and never leave the window open.
dlg @1 master.wages : There's no wage in learning. You work alongside me and it sinks in faster, and whatever you turn out, you sell for yourself.
dlg @1 master.takes [here.take_on=yes] : A single learner, somebody I'm sure of. I'm sure of you. Say the word.
dlg @2 master.takes [here.take_on=no] : A single learner, somebody I'm sure of. #here.take_on_why#
dlg @1 master.taken : Good. Start tomorrow, early. Shut the window as you come in.
dlg @1 master.refused : #topic.why# I'm sorry. | Not yet. #topic.why#
dlg @1 master.bye : #farewell#. Think it over. | #farewell#. Come back when you've decided.
graft resident.hub
choice Got a riddle for me? -> riddle
node riddle
say Always. Born on a back, raised on a wheel, I end up on another back. What am I? Take your time. Quick answers are mostly wrong.
mood pleased
free
choice Wool? -> riddle_answer
choice Back to the counter. -> hub
node riddle_answer
say Wool. The sheep's back, the spinning wheel, then a coat on your back. You took your time. Good.
choice Another time. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft master.learn
choice Why keep the window shut? -> shut
node shut
say Damp gets into the wool and mildews it. And the flies. Mostly the flies. Nothing to do with my chest, before you ask.
free
choice Your chest? -> shut_chest
choice Back to the lesson. -> learn
node shut_chest
say My chest is perfectly well. It's the lint from the wool. Now, are you learning or not?
mood stern
choice Back to the lesson. -> learn
choice Goodbye. -> bye
